<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lola Montez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Eliza Gilbert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lola Montez was the stage name of Eliza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a>, an Irish-born dancer and actress who presented herself as Spanish. In 1846 she became the favourite of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a>, who made her Countess of Landsfeld; her influence and arrogance provoked public anger that merged with the revolution of 1848 and forced Ludwig to abdicate. She later performed in America and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lola-montez.jpg" alt="Lola Montez" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Samuel Adam-Salomon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine-Samuel_Adam-Salomon_-_Lola_Montez_c1860_-_(MeisterDrucke-148433).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eliza Rosanna Gilbert was born into an Anglo-Irish family, the daughter of Ensign Edward Gilbert and Eliza Oliver. Her father's regiment took the family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1823, where he died of cholera, and her mother soon married Lieutenant Patrick Craigie. The child was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to school in Montrose, Sunderland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>, and was known as a mischief-maker. In 1837, at sixteen, she eloped with Lieutenant Thomas James; they separated five years later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, and she became a professional dancer under a stage name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> debut in June 1843 as Lola Montez, the Spanish dancer, was spoiled when she was recognised as Mrs James, so she left for the continent, where she had success in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. She had a disappointing Paris debut in 1844 in Fromental Halévy's opera Le lazzarone, and moved in literary circles that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>, George Sand and Alexandre Dumas. In 1846 she arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and became the mistress of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a>.</p><p>Ludwig made her Countess of Landsfeld and Baroness of Rosenthal on 25 August 1847 and gave her a large annuity. For more than a year she used her influence in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>. The minister of state, Karl von Abel, was dismissed with his cabinet after objecting to her Bavarian nationality and title, and at her insistence the king closed the university. In March 1848, under revolutionary pressure, the university was reopened, Ludwig abdicated in favour of his son Maximilian II, and she fled.</p><p>She went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, married George Trafford Heald, and had to flee a bigamy action because her divorce from James forbade remarriage. In 1851 she went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and in 1853 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, where she married the newspaperman Patrick Hull and settled at Grass Valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. There she gave dancing lessons to the young Lotta Crabtree. In 1855 she toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, where her Spider Dance scandalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballarat">Ballarat</a> in February 1856 she attacked the editor Henry Seekamp with a whip after a bad review.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in America she failed to revive her stage career but found a new one as a lecturer, speaking on topics such as the Wits and Women of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and as a writer, publishing The Arts of Beauty. In her last years she worked among women trying to leave prostitution. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 17 January 1861, though accounts differ on the cause and her age, and she is buried in Green-Wood Cemetery in Brooklyn. Her story has inspired novels and several films, and some writers have seen her as a source for Irene Adler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conan-doyle">Arthur Conan Doyle</a>'s Sherlock Holmes story A Scandal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lola Montez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His affair with the dancer <b>Lola Montez</b> discredited him, and he abdicated in March 1848 in favour of his son Maximilian II, who accepted liberal reforms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Grange, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.4,-8.52&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Grange on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4362 300 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lola Montez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4812 570h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4812 570h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.4,-8.52&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
